WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.368
A merchant dashboard reads a table of revenue per day.

2
00:00:06.368 --> 00:00:11.055
A job called v1 keeps that table current by reading the payments log.

3
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:16.504
Then finance finds a bug: v1 never subtracts refunds, so revenue is too high.

4
00:00:16.504 --> 00:00:22.221
The fix needs the refunds, and v1 never stored them, so its table can't be patched.

5
00:00:22.433 --> 00:00:27.637
The obvious fix is to stop v1, deploy the new code, and rebuild the table in place.

6
00:00:27.637 --> 00:00:35.161
For sixteen minutes every merchant sees a half built table, and if the new code is wrong, there's nothing to go back to.

7
00:00:35.333 --> 00:00:39.323
The log still holds all thirty days, so there's a safer way.

8
00:00:39.323 --> 00:00:42.249
Start a second job, v2, with the fixed code.

9
00:00:42.249 --> 00:00:45.508
It reads from offset zero and writes a new table.

10
00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:49.675
v1 keeps running the whole time, so readers notice nothing.

11
00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:54.078
v2 reads four hundred records a minute while five new ones arrive.

12
00:00:54.078 --> 00:00:58.447
Halfway through, its table has the first two weeks and nothing after.

13
00:00:58.667 --> 00:01:00.245
So nobody reads v2 yet.

14
00:01:00.245 --> 00:01:04.227
A merchant moved now would see the last two weeks as zero.

15
00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:10.220
Six thousand records, gaining three hundred and ninety five a minute, take sixteen minutes.

16
00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:16.040
Then v2 reads the last record, and both tables follow the same log, each with its own code.

17
00:01:16.233 --> 00:01:22.795
Now move readers over, a stable share at a time: a quarter of the merchants first, always the same ones.

18
00:01:22.795 --> 00:01:27.148
Their numbers should differ from v1 by the refunds, and nothing else.

19
00:01:27.333 --> 00:01:32.012
If v2 shows a wrong number, send everyone back to v1 with one switch.

20
00:01:32.012 --> 00:01:36.691
That works because v1 never stopped, so its table is just as current.

21
00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:43.628
Once every merchant is on v2 and it has run quietly for a while, stop v1 and drop its table.

22
00:01:43.628 --> 00:01:45.895
Only this last step is one way.

23
00:01:46.067 --> 00:01:53.753
Keep the log long enough, and changing a derived table is a replay into a new one, while the old one serves until you switch.
